WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.380
These were not the plot holes and goofy&nbsp;
voices you were looking for. Here are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:09.145
some Jedi mind tricks Star Wars played to alter&nbsp;
fans' memories of the franchise, for the better.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.800
Even after all these years, The Phantom&nbsp;
Menace continues to rank as one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.820
lesser entries in the Star Wars saga.&nbsp;
Elements of the film are certainly strong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.380
with the visuals alone aging remarkably&nbsp;
well in the two decades since its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.720
but one noticeably weak aspect of the initial&nbsp;
film is the puppet used to portray Yoda.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.260
As had been the case in the original&nbsp;
trilogy, Yoda was initially featured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.220
on screen through the use of puppetry.&nbsp;
Despite the movie's massive budget, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.480
the puppet version of Yoda that appeared in&nbsp;
the first version of The Phantom Menace was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:45.000
somehow of far worse quality than the one&nbsp;
previously used in Lucas' original trilogy.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.560
Fortunately, Lucas patched this up when it&nbsp;
came time for Attack of the Clones. Realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:54.000
that Yoda would need to physically move around&nbsp;
more in his lightsaber battle with Count Dooku,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.740
Lucas reverted to a CGI iteration of the Jedi&nbsp;
Master, one that would appear throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:03.480
final two films of the prequel trilogy. To&nbsp;
smooth the continuity between the three films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.260
he also went back and removed the puppet&nbsp;
version of Yoda from The Phantom Menace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.543
digitally replacing him with the&nbsp;
CGI version of the character.

00:01:10.697 --> 00:01:15.480
One of the most popular changes George&nbsp;
Lucas made to The Empire Strikes Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.680
was the insertion of Ian McDiarmid's&nbsp;
portrayal of Emperor Palpatine into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.220
the re-released cut of the film. In the&nbsp;
original release, the Emperor briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.380
appears in holographic form to converse with&nbsp;
Darth Vader. Voiced by New Zealand stage actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:32.400
Clive Revill and physically portrayed&nbsp;
by Marjorie Eaton, this version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.720
Emperor looked and sounded nothing like the&nbsp;
Emperor fans later saw in Return of the Jedi.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:44.192
"The Force is strong with him. The son&nbsp;
of Skywalker must not become a Jedi."

00:01:44.192 --> 00:01:48.660
In fact, the design of Palpatine here is often&nbsp;
considered one of the rare flaws within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:53.400
original cut of The Empire Strikes Back. When it&nbsp;
came time for Lucas' recut of the film decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.660
later, he removed this rudimentary version&nbsp;
of Palpatine and replaced it with McDiarmid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.080
the actor who portrayed Palpatine&nbsp;
across all three Star Wars trilogies.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:05.400
It's one of the few changes Lucas made to the&nbsp;
original trilogy that fans almost universally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.780
support, improving the Emperor's appearance&nbsp;
in The Empire Strikes Back tenfold. The new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.560
version not only helps link the original&nbsp;
and prequel trilogies together, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.180
gives viewers another chance to see McDiarmid&nbsp;
doing his thing — and that's never a bad thing.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.860
One of the cleverer changes made to&nbsp;
Star Wars is a viable explanation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.640
behind the Imperial stormtroopers'&nbsp;
almost impossibly poor aim. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.020
Obi-Wan Kenobi describes the stormtroopers&nbsp;
as highly accurate marksmen in A New Hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.400
everyone knows that they are anything but. In&nbsp;
fact, the stormtroopers' aim in the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.780
trilogy is so awful that "stormtrooper&nbsp;
aim" has become more or less the go-to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.560
description when it comes to villains who are&nbsp;
unable to shoot their enemies in films or TV.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.240
In more recent years, Disney has helped provide&nbsp;
a feasible reason why stormtroopers' aims are so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.460
inexplicably bad. In various shows and comics,&nbsp;
it has been established that the Empire began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.180
cutting costs after seizing control of&nbsp;
the galaxy — including slashing military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.440
funding. Unable to afford proper equipment&nbsp;
for its troops, the Empire began outfitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.460
its soldiers with largely ineffective,&nbsp;
poorly-constructed weapons and armor.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.220
This retcon helps explain why stormtroopers' armor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.220
is essentially useless when it comes&nbsp;
to projectiles or blunt weapons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.820
as well as why stormtroopers are&nbsp;
unable to hit targets in close range.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.200
Another change made in the remastered&nbsp;
version of The Empire Strikes Back is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.580
the removal of Jason Wingreen's vocal&nbsp;
performance as Boba Fett. While British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.660
actor Jeremy Bulloch wore the armor&nbsp;
of the famous bounty hunter on screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:32.400
Wingreen dubbed Fett's lines with an almost&nbsp;
guttural, Clint Eastwood-esque growl.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.300
"What if he doesn't survive?&nbsp;
He's worth a lot to me."

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.560
While certainly intimidating enough, Lucas chose&nbsp;
to replace Wingreen's vocal performance with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.120
Temuera Morrison, the actor who portrayed&nbsp;
Jango Fett in the prequel trilogy. As Boba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.740
himself is a literal clone of Jango, it makes&nbsp;
sense that Lucas would have Morrison voice the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:53.940
character in the remastered version of The Empire&nbsp;
Strikes Back. Some fans might miss the original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:58.800
harshly-delivered snarl of Wingreen's character,&nbsp;
but the switch certainly makes sense in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:04.140
of continuity. Additionally, Lucas inserted&nbsp;
Boba into the 1997 rerelease of A New Hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:09.060
digitally placing him alongside Jabba the Hutt on&nbsp;
Tatooine. It's a minor change, but one that helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:13.140
establish Fett's professional relationship&nbsp;
with Jabba early in the original trilogy.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:18.000
In spite of his incredibly limited screen&nbsp;
time, Boba Fett became a fan-favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.360
breakout character after his initial&nbsp;
appearance in The Empire Strikes Back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.800
When it came time for his return in Return of&nbsp;
the Jedi, however, the character infamously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.140
met an unceremonious and downright goofy end,&nbsp;
falling into the Sarlacc pit early in the film.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:34.680
Despite seemingly meeting his&nbsp;
death, Fett was hastily restored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.280
to life thanks to a number of "Legends"&nbsp;
comics that expanded on the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.180
revealing that he survived his encounter&nbsp;
with the Sarlacc. With Disney's acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.380
of Lucasfilm in 2012, though, all of&nbsp;
these stories were rendered non-canon.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.180
But it was only a matter of time before newer Star&nbsp;
Wars media confirmed Fett's survival, leading to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:55.500
number of appearances in The Mandalorian and in&nbsp;
his own spin-off series, The Book of Boba Fett.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.960
Given her prominent role in The Clone Wars,&nbsp;
it's easy to forget that aspiring Sith acolyte&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:07.080
Asajj Ventress actually made her debut in 2003's&nbsp;
underrated Clone Wars animated series by Genndy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.900
Tartakovsky — albeit in a massively reduced&nbsp;
role. In Tartakovsky's series, Ventress appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.880
as a one-off Force-wielder recruited by Count&nbsp;
Dooku to assassinate Anakin Skywalker. Dueling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:22.680
the Jedi in the jungles of Yavin 4, Ventress&nbsp;
tests Anakin's physical and emotional limits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.000
narrowly beating him before a rage-filled&nbsp;
Anakin sends her hurtling to her death.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:31.920
In the 2008 Clone Wars computer-animated film,&nbsp;
a different version of Ventress appears in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:36.420
more expanded role, as an antagonist to&nbsp;
the Jedi and a commander of the Separatist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:40.140
Army. While initially similar in nature&nbsp;
to the original version of the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.980
Ventress would become more fully fleshed&nbsp;
out as The Clone Wars went on, receiving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.660
her own backstory and eventual redemption arc&nbsp;
after she is betrayed by her former master,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:53.580
Dooku. Before long, Ventress became one of the&nbsp;
key players in the latter-day Clone Wars series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:57.358
with many fans seeing her as one&nbsp;
of the show's best characters.

00:05:57.358 --> 00:06:03.000
One of the earliest and most glaringly obvious&nbsp;
plot holes in 1977's Star Wars was how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.680
Empire overlooked such a crucial flaw in the&nbsp;
Death Star plans as its critical exhaust port.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.280
"The target area is only two meters wide. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.340
small thermal exhaust port,&nbsp;
right below the main port."

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.240
As seen in the movie, all the Rebels&nbsp;
need to do is launch one proton torpedo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.780
into the port in order to destroy&nbsp;
the entire battle station. It's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.900
oversight on the Empire's part that leads&nbsp;
directly to the Death Star's destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:29.100
leaving many wondering how they could&nbsp;
possibly miss such a devastating weakness.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:33.780
With the release of Rogue One nearly four decades&nbsp;
later, however, it's established that the exhaust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.240
port was actually put there on purpose by Galen&nbsp;
Erso. Forced to build the Death Star by Orson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.560
Krennic and knowing the devastating effect&nbsp;
it will have on the galaxy, Erso designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:48.180
the Death Star with one crucial weakness that&nbsp;
the Empire's enemies will be able to exploit.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:53.520
Showing Erso's efforts to sabotage the station&nbsp;
through the exhaust port was an effortless way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.660
of clearing up confusion around one of the&nbsp;
original trilogy's most questionable plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.200
points. It also makes the ending of&nbsp;
A New Hope that much more satisfying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:05.400
knowing the crucial part that Erso — and his&nbsp;
daughter — played in the Death Star's destruction.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:10.020
A chief complaint fans had regarding&nbsp;
The Phantom Menace was the sudden and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.580
surprising death of Darth Maul. Possessing&nbsp;
few lines and a very limited screen presence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:18.660
Maul was nevertheless seen as a villain&nbsp;
with enough visual appeal and menace to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:23.160
rival Darth Vader. As such, many believed he&nbsp;
might fill a role similar to Vader's in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:27.600
prequel trilogy. When he met his abrupt&nbsp;
end in the climactic duel with Obi-Wan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:31.380
however, some audience members saw his&nbsp;
early demise as a wasted opportunity.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:36.420
Knowing fans' love for the Sith warrior, Lucas&nbsp;
made the decision to revive Maul in The Clone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:41.460
Wars. In the show, it's explained that Maul&nbsp;
miraculously survived his maiming at Obi-Wan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:45.480
hands, although he was left physically and&nbsp;
mentally broken by the ordeal. When his brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:49.860
Savage Opress, rescues him from a junk&nbsp;
planet he'd been stranded on for a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:53.700
he helps restore Maul's mind. Fully&nbsp;
restored, Maul leads his brother in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:58.500
forging a new criminal empire in the galaxy,&nbsp;
becoming a perpetual thorn in the Jedi's side.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.760
The decision to reverse Maul's death was&nbsp;
a welcome one in the eyes of longtime Star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:06.300
Wars fans. Maul's expanded role in The&nbsp;
Clone Wars and Rebels has been praised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.900
as one of the highlights of both shows,&nbsp;
helping expand the character and take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.420
advantage of his unique chemistry&nbsp;
with the show's other characters.

00:08:13.334 --> 00:08:17.160
"Execute Order 66."

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:21.000
One of the hardest things George Lucas&nbsp;
and Dave Filoni had to explain in The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:25.500
Clone Wars was how the clone troopers could&nbsp;
so easily turn on their Jedi commanders. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:29.220
shown in the series, the clones are usually&nbsp;
close friends and confidantes to the Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.800
bonding with them throughout the&nbsp;
war. This makes their dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:35.520
shift in personality during Order 66&nbsp;
that much more difficult to believe.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.840
Nevertheless, Lucas and Filoni came up&nbsp;
with an ingenious idea that provided a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:43.800
feasible explanation behind the clones'&nbsp;
sudden betrayal. In the prequel trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:48.360
the clones' nonchalant massacre of the Jedi&nbsp;
is done of their own volition. The clones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:51.660
seem to genuinely believe Palpatine's&nbsp;
lie that the Jedi are taking over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:55.680
choosing to remain loyal to the Chancellor&nbsp;
and the Republic over the Jedi conspirators.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:59.940
However, this would later be retconned in the&nbsp;
final few seasons of The Clone Wars, with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:05.100
show revealing that nearly every clone trooper is&nbsp;
implanted with a behavioral modification biochip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:09.240
that effectively controls their thoughts and&nbsp;
emotions. When activated, they essentially become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:13.680
sleeper agents, instilling them with dramatically&nbsp;
high aggression against their Jedi comrades.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.820
Arguably one of the best retcons made in The&nbsp;
Clone Wars, the biochips explain the sudden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:21.780
shift in personality among the clones, and&nbsp;
helps portray them in a new, tragic light.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.100
One of the biggest changes made to the&nbsp;
Star Wars universe was the introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:30.540
of Ahsoka Tano in The Clone Wars. Although she&nbsp;
doesn't appear in any of the prequel movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.260
Lucas and Filoni did a wonderful job&nbsp;
interweaving the character's narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:38.340
into the overall universe of the prequels.&nbsp;
Over time, Ahsoka would become one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:42.900
most beloved characters in the entire Star&nbsp;
Wars canon, her popularity directly leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.200
to appearances in other shows, including&nbsp;
her own spin-off series on Disney+.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:51.420
Aside from her frequent appearances following The&nbsp;
Clone Wars, Ahsoka also allowed a more layered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:56.400
exploration of Anakin's character. Gradually&nbsp;
learning to become a more mature master to Ahsoka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:01.320
Anakin was finally able to realize the burden of&nbsp;
having a pupil study under him. The Jedi Order's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.860
poor treatment of Ahsoka also provided&nbsp;
an additional reason for his frustrations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:09.360
with the Jedi in Revenge of the Sith, helping&nbsp;
shed more light on his turn to the Dark Side.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:13.260
It's up for debate whether George Lucas&nbsp;
had a complete vision for the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:18.000
trilogy in his mind while working on Star&nbsp;
Wars in the mid-1970s. There are certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.600
some issues with the canon that support&nbsp;
the idea that Lucas was still planning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:25.680
out the story as he developed the second&nbsp;
installment of the original trilogy. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:29.160
most obvious among these issues would be&nbsp;
the reveal that Leia and Luke are siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.880
and the revelation that Darth Vader is&nbsp;
the father of the two long-lost twins.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:37.200
In early drafts of The Empire Strikes Back,&nbsp;
Vader appears as a separate character from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:42.900
Anakin Skywalker, who appears as a Force ghost&nbsp;
to Luke on Dagobah. In that scene, Anakin's ghost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:47.040
reveals to Luke that he has a sister, although&nbsp;
he refuses to share any further information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:51.900
for fear of Darth Vader finding out about her.&nbsp;
The fact that Anakin and Vader are two separate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:56.432
characters falls in line with Obi-Wan telling&nbsp;
Luke in A New Hope that Vader killed Anakin.

00:10:56.432 --> 00:10:58.500
"He betrayed and murdered your father."

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:01.680
In this rough draft of the script,&nbsp;
Luke's sister is called Nellith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.800
and it seems that she is meant to be a&nbsp;
separate character from Leia. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.580
Lucas would decide to tweak both of these&nbsp;
decisions, turning Leia into Luke's sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:13.260
and making Vader their father — resulting in one&nbsp;
of the greatest plot twists in cinematic history.

